# Reading NotePlan plugin logs

Use `nplog` (in `scripts/nplog/`) to read NotePlan's plugin log. Run it as
`node scripts/nplog/nplog` from the repo root, or as `nplog` after a one-time
`./scripts/nplog/install.sh`.

## Never grep the raw log file

All three obvious greps return **empty**, and empty reads as "no errors", which is worse
than an error:

- `grep '^JSLog:'` matches nothing. Every line has a timestamp before the marker.
- `grep '| ERROR |'` matches nothing. WARN and ERROR use emoji delimiters
  (`🥺 WARN 🥺`, `❗️ ERROR ❗️`), not pipes. See `LOG_LEVEL_STRINGS` in `helpers/dev.js`.
- Filtering on the leading timestamp is wrong on about two thirds of lines, by up to
  hours. That is the flush time, not the event time.

A pretty-printed object also spans a dozen physical lines with only the first carrying the
marker, so grepping a field hands back an orphan.

## Use --json for programmatic reading

The default mode is a full-screen interactive viewer and will hang a non-TTY caller.

```bash
node scripts/nplog/nplog --json --last-run          # the most recent plugin invocation
node scripts/nplog/nplog --since 10m --json         # last 10 minutes (also 30s, 2h, 19:36)
node scripts/nplog/nplog --since 10m --json --min-level warn
node scripts/nplog/nplog --json --mode 10 '\[DIAG\]'  # a match plus the next 10 entries
```

Output is NDJSON, one object per entry then a summary. Each record carries `ts`, `level`
(debug/info/warn/error), `source` (plugin/webview), `run` (which `Executing function`
invocation it belongs to, so an error can be attributed to the call that caused it), and
`text` (the whole entry, objects intact).

**Exit code 1 means at least one emitted entry was an ERROR.** Check that before reading
anything.

## After triggering an action

```bash
CURSOR=$(node scripts/nplog/nplog --mark)
open "noteplan://x-callback-url/runPlugin?pluginID=jgclark.Dashboard&command=Show%20Dashboard"
node scripts/nplog/nplog --since "$CURSOR" --follow --wait-idle 5 --json
```

An x-callback returns immediately while the plugin keeps running, and a Dashboard refresh
ping-pongs asynchronously for anything from 2 to 30 seconds, so do not guess a duration.
`--follow` streams entries as they arrive; `--wait-idle 5` stops after five seconds of
silence, which is a sound "finished" signal because a run still in flight writes something
within that window.

**Never read the log immediately after firing an action.** It flushes in batches. Measured
on a live system: the file sat untouched for 24 seconds, then gained 80 lines at once.
`--wait-idle` will not report "done" before it has seen output, and the summary includes
`sawOutput` and `timedOut` so "the action logged nothing" is distinguishable from "I looked
too early". Do not set `--timeout` below about 30s (default is 90).

## Do not use the per-plugin log as evidence

`Plugins/<id>/_MCP-console.log`, which the NotePlan MCP's `noteplan_plugins action:"log"`
reads, is truncated on every plugin invocation. For a plugin that refreshes on a timer such
as the Dashboard, an unrelated background refresh wipes the run under investigation within
seconds. Measured for the same second: 32 unique lines there against 66 in the main log.
The main log is a strict superset, and `nplog` reads only that.

Full docs in `scripts/nplog/README.md`; parser internals in `scripts/nplog/AGENTS.md`.
